# Trained weight files are written by every run INCLUDING smoke tests, so
# they are not tracked by default — an 8-genome, 1-generation smoke result
# sitting in the repo looks exactly like a real one, and the whole point of
# weights.js refusing an uncommented non-zero is that a number has to carry
# the run that earned it.
#
# A result worth keeping gets committed deliberately, with its provenance
# in the commit message: git add -f trained.<mode>.json
trained.*.json

# The generic name is SCRATCH. Every run overwrites trained.<mode>.json,
# including the run you started five minutes ago, so a result kept under
# that name is a result waiting to be silently replaced — and it showed up
# as a phantom "uncommitted change" every time a new run finished, which is
# noise that trains you to ignore the warning.
#
# A result worth keeping is renamed: trained.<mode>.<round>.json, committed
# with -f and with its provenance in the commit message. Those are tracked
# and never overwritten by a run.
!trained.*.round*.json
